Given the following base contract:

```solidity
contract Base {
    uint256 public something = 0;

    function nop() public pure {}

    function set(uint256 val) public {
        something = val;
    }
}
```
And the following case `A`:
```solidity
contract A {
    function a() public pure {
        Base.nop();
    }
}
```
Solang compiles this just fine. Furthermore, consider the following case `B`:
```solidity
contract B {
    function b() public {
        Base.set(1);
    }
}
```
Solang crashes during compilation (target either `substrate` or `solana`):
```
thread 'main' panicked at 'get_storage_slot called on non-storage variable', src/codegen/cfg.rs:1879:13
note: run with `RUST_BACKTRACE=1` environment variable to display a backtrace
```
Where as `solc` behaves more in a way I'd expect. For both cases it gets the same error:
```
❯ solc --bin foo.sol
Error: Cannot call function via contract type name.
  --> foo.sol:27:9:
   |
17 |         Base.nop();
   |         ^^^^^^^^^^

Error: Cannot call function via contract type name.
  --> foo.sol:33:9:
   |
23 |         Base.set(1);
   |         ^^^^^^^^^^^
```
